---
title: "stdlib.set: Sets as Canonical Lists"
author: Lateralus contributors
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: Build deterministic sets with the standard library's sorted, deduplicated list representation.
---

# Why a list represents a set

`stdlib.set` keeps sets simple and deterministic: every set is a sorted list with duplicates removed. That makes values easy to print, compare, serialize, and use in tests.

## Create and update a set

```lateralus
import stdlib.set

let tools = set.from_list(["compiler", "vm", "compiler"])
let expanded = set.add(tools, "linter")
println(expanded) // ["compiler", "linter", "vm"]
```

`from_list` is the normal boundary for incoming data. `add` is idempotent, so adding an existing value leaves the set unchanged. `remove` returns another canonical set.

## Combine sets

```lateralus
let frontend = set.from_list(["lexer", "parser"])
let backend = set.from_list(["parser", "vm"])

println(set.union(frontend, backend))
println(set.intersection(frontend, backend))
println(set.difference(frontend, backend))
```

The result of each set operation is sorted and deduplicated. Use `has`, `is_subset`, `is_superset`, and `equals` for predicates; `to_list` makes the representation explicit when passing a set to another API.

## A useful mental model

Treat a set value as an immutable result rather than an in-place container:

```lateralus
let original = set.from_list([3, 1, 2])
let next = set.add(original, 4)
// original is [1, 2, 3]; next is [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

This canonical representation is the module's contract and is pinned by the runnable example and test vectors in the repository.
